{"id":"n-20261007-f3e39t","category":"world","display_title":"2026 Nobel Prizes: Physics and Economics Predictions","display_title_he":"פרסי נובל 2026: פיזיקה ותחזיות לכלכלה","short_summary":"Belgian-born physicist Francis Halzen wins the 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics for the IceCube Neutrino Observatory, while prediction markets highlight top contenders for the upcoming Economics prize.","english_level":"C1","words":507,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Nobel_Pierre_et_Marie_Curie_1.jpg/960px-Nobel_Pierre_et_Marie_Curie_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sofia Gisberg , uploaded and retouched by Jebulon · Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nobel_Pierre_et_Marie_Curie_1.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"NPR","domain":"npr.org","url":"https://www.npr.org/sections/planet-money/2026/10/07/g-s1-146828/who-will-win-the-2026-nobel-prize-in-economics","title":"Who will win the 2026 Nobel Prize in economics?","published_at":"2026-10-07T10:30:00.000Z"},{"name":"Ars Technica","domain":"arstechnica.com","url":"https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/neutrino-physicist-wins-2026-nobel-physics-prize","title":"Neutrino physicist wins 2026 Nobel Physics Prize","published_at":"2026-10-06T16:53:17.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-06T16:53:17.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-07T13:49:04.455Z","quality_score":10,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The 2026 Nobel Prize season has arrived, bringing both historic celebrations of scientific triumph and lively intellectual speculation. In physics, the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences awarded the prestigious prize to Francis Halzen, the visionary driving force behind the IceCube Neutrino Observatory. Meanwhile, in the realm of economics, enthusiasts are turning to prediction markets to guess who will receive the world's most coveted wake-up call from Stockholm—officially known as the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel, established in 1968 by Sweden's central bank.","Halzen, a Belgian-born physicist at the University of Wisconsin-Madison, was honored for his audacious idea to transform a massive volume of Antarctic ice into a giant telescope. Neutrinos, dubbed \"ghost particles\" by Isaac Asimov because they have almost no mass and rarely interact with matter, travel near the speed of light. Joanne Hewett, a colleague, recalled Halzen's sudden inspiration during a cold winter day looking out over frozen Lake Mendota: \"In the elevator... Francis exclaimed, 'You could do it in ice!'\" To capture these particles, Halzen proposed burying light sensors deep within the ultra-pure ice of the South Pole. Despite early skepticism from \"respectable conservative\" physicists, Halzen and his collaborators successfully constructed IceCube, which features 5,160 optical sensors suspended along 86 cables a kilometer deep. Since coming online in 2011, the observatory has revolutionized astronomy, detecting high-energy neutrinos—including those nicknamed Bert, Ernie, and Big Bird—from distant galaxies.","While the physics community celebrates a concrete triumph, the economics community is engaged in a classic academic pastime: forecasting. To predict the winner, some look to prediction markets like Kalshi, reflecting Friedrich von Hayek's 1974 Nobel-winning idea that prices aggregate scattered information. However, this specific market remains incredibly \"thin,\" recording only about $11,000 in trading volume compared to the $670 million recorded for Kalshi's 2024 presidential election market. Nonetheless, the fluctuating rankings highlight several prominent scholars whose work has shaped modern policy.","At the top of the prediction rankings is Ariel Pakes of Harvard University. Alongside Steven Berry and James Levinsohn, Pakes developed the landmark \"BLP model\" in 1995 to study the American automobile industry. Today, antitrust regulators use this empirical tool to estimate how corporate mergers might affect consumer prices. Another top contender is Susan Athey of Stanford Graduate School of Business, a pioneer in digital technology economics and the first woman to win the John Bates Clark Medal. Athey has designed markets to remove atmospheric carbon and pioneered the use of machine learning to answer causal questions, while also actively campaigning against discrimination and harassment within the profession.","Rounding out the top predictions is Harvard's Robert Barro, renowned for his \"Ricardian equivalence\" theory, which suggests that debt-funded tax cuts may not stimulate spending because forward-looking citizens anticipate future tax increases. Other notable contenders include Thomas Piketty and Emmanuel Saez for their pathbreaking research on wealth inequality, and Janet Currie for her work on how childhood disadvantages shape long-term economic outcomes. Whether through the precise detection of subatomic particles or the noisy aggregation of market predictions, the search for groundbreaking knowledge remains a cornerstone of global progress."],"facts":[{"fact":"Belgian-born physicist Francis Halzen won the 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics for his work on the IceCube Neutrino Observatory.","sources":[2]},{"fact":"The IceCube Neutrino Observatory features over 5,000 optical sensors buried deep in Antarctic ice to detect high-energy neutrinos.","sources":[2]},{"fact":"Prediction markets like Kalshi are being used to forecast the 2026 Nobel Prize in Economics, though trading volume is low.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Top contenders in the economics prediction market include Ariel Pakes, Susan Athey, and Robert Barro.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"prestigious","lemma":"prestigious","pos":"adj","he":"יוקרתי","name":false},{"text":"visionary","lemma":"visionary","pos":"adj","he":"חזוני / בעל חזון","name":false},{"text":"driving force"